last week we established that Coors is  more misunderstood than full-on monster  and today we're diving a bit deeper  we'll be learning about two types of  Korres requests and configuration as you  might remember we left our heroes as  they narrowly avoided a Korres error and  now they must learn to differentiate  between the different types of Korres  requests there are two types of course  requests simple and preflighted a simple  request can be initiated directly that  simple a preflighted request must send a  preliminary pre-flight request to the  server to get permission before the  primary requests can proceed that is the  client has to ask and the server has to  give the green light before the data can  be served  there are several circumstances under  which request is preflighted it uses  methods other than get ahead or post it  uses the post method with a content type  other than these types or it sets custom  headers for example if your app uses put  to ensure idempotent requests this  request would need to be preflighted  let's look at what happens when a  browser makes a simple request to cloud  storage the browser adds origin header  to the request the origin header  contains as you might have guessed the  origin of the resource seeking to share  the other domains resources cloud  storage compares the HTTP method of the  request like get or post and the value  of the origin header to the methods and  origins information in the target cloud  storage bucket scores configuration to  see if there are matches if there are  cloud storage origin includes the access  control allow origin header and it's  response the access control allow origin  header contains the value of the origin  header from the initial request the  browser then receives the response and  checks to see if the access control  allow origin value matches the domain  specified in the original request if  they do match the request succeeds if  they don't match or if the access  control allow origin header is  not present in the response the request  fails a preflighted request performs  some additional steps first before  following the same steps in a simple  request first the browser sends an  options request containing the requested  method and requested headers of the  primary request the server responds back  with the values of the HTTP methods and  headers allowed by the targeted resource  if any of the method or header values in  the pre-flight request aren't in the set  of methods and headers allowed by the  targeted resource the request fails and  the primary request isn't sent if the  method or header values are included the  request continues like a simple Korres  request once again this is a true bite  of the full course meal so make sure you  check out the documentation to learn  some more but let's talk about how you  configure cores for cloud storage cloud  storage allows you to set cores  configuration at the bucket level only  you can set the course configuration for  a bucket using the gsutil command-line  tool the XML API or the JSON API let's  look at setting up cores configuration  with gsutil  first I need a cores JSON document  here's an example this JSON document  explicitly allows cross-origin get and  post requests from origin 1 example.com  but not head it may include the content  type response header then I set the  course policy of the bucket to the JSON  document to remove the cores  configuration of a bucket the GS util  command is the same the difference is  that the cores JSON document is empty  now you know a bit more about cores as  well as how to set the cores  configuration for a cloud storage bucket  if you want to know even more about  cloud storage and cores check out the  links in the description don't forget to  subscribe give us a like and tell us  what you want to learn about Google  Cloud storage thanks for joining us for  this quick byte of cloud storage we'll  see you next time  [Music]
 

